1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
She is the cradle of Western civilization.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
The birthplace of democracy, art, and philosophy.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
But construction and excavation threaten to destroy her past.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,000
This could have been the disaster of the millennium.

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
One of the largest engineering and archaeological feats in Europe

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
is digging through layers of ancient history,

7
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
transforming Athens into a 21st century metropolis.

8
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, the Athens subway on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,280
Greece.

10
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:49,660
This beautiful mountainous country boasts hundreds of islands and 9,000 miles of shoreline.

11
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:58,640
Athens, her capital, sits on Attica, a peninsula about 40 miles wide and 50 miles long.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,600
Ringed by mountains, high-rises share the Athens skyline with crumbling ancient structures.

13
00:01:08,020 --> 00:01:11,420
Here, the ancient and modern live side by side.

14
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:17,200
Cars drive past classical triumphal arches that have survived millennia.

15
00:01:21,740 --> 00:01:26,680
Over the last 30 years, the population of Athens has doubled to nearly 4 million.

16
00:01:26,681 --> 00:01:32,100
One and a half million people live in the dense, busy heart of the city,

17
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:35,480
compounding its long-standing reputation for car pollution,

18
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:41,240
known to erode the smooth surface of marble, damaging ancient buildings and statues.

19
00:01:42,180 --> 00:01:48,180
If you think of the classical antiquities of Athens from the 5th century B.C.,

20
00:01:48,181 --> 00:02:01,180
Those antiquities suffered less, really, over 2,500 years than they did in 25 years of automobiles circulating in the city of Athens.

21
00:02:01,540 --> 00:02:06,780
Many Athenians struggled to work on Line 1, Athens' antiquated urban train system.

22
00:02:08,180 --> 00:02:13,760
It is a single line, which only runs between Piraeus on the coast and Kephysia in the north.

23
00:02:13,761 --> 00:02:24,240
Transport in an urban setting is vital to the quality of life of every major city.

24
00:02:24,940 --> 00:02:26,080
Athens is not an exception.

25
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:34,940
On top of this daily demand, the return of the modern Olympics to Athens in 2004

26
00:02:34,941 --> 00:02:40,100
created an enormous influx of people who were required to use public transportation

27
00:02:40,101 --> 00:02:42,480
to and from the Olympic facilities.

28
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:50,420
Everybody was recognizing the need to improve the public transport system.

29
00:02:50,421 --> 00:02:56,280
Everybody knew that this could not be done by constructing more roads.

30
00:02:56,281 --> 00:03:00,720
And Line 1 could not cope with the growing demands of a modern city.

31
00:03:00,721 --> 00:03:04,700
A modern solution was desperately needed.

32
00:03:04,701 --> 00:03:09,880
Modern Olympics, which were reinvented at the end of the 19th century, only exist because

33
00:03:09,881 --> 00:03:12,240
there were ancient Olympics.

34
00:03:12,241 --> 00:03:16,980
And so in this interesting twist, it's ancient history, the ancient Olympics,

35
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:21,660
that leads to the modern Olympics, that leads to the technological marvel of the Athens Metro.

36
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:26,380
The plan called for two new underground lines.

37
00:03:29,420 --> 00:03:33,740
Attico Metro's Lines 2 and 3 would intersect with the existing Line 1,

38
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:37,560
providing 12 more miles of line and 20 new stations.

39
00:03:37,561 --> 00:03:43,920
The original schedule for constructing the Athens Metro, when the contract was signed

40
00:03:43,921 --> 00:03:50,040
with this French, German and Greek consortium, was for completion by the end of 1997.

41
00:03:50,820 --> 00:03:58,020
In 1993, Athens residents watched as the two gigantic tunnel boring machines, or TBMs,

42
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:04,000
nicknamed Metro Mouse, arrived to bore tunnels under one of the densest modern cities in

43
00:04:04,001 --> 00:04:04,701
the world.

44
00:04:04,702 --> 00:04:11,980
the TBMs would have to dig under some of the most recognizable architecture of the ancient world

45
00:04:11,981 --> 00:04:18,360
through ground containing some of the oldest artifacts of western civilization not everyone

46
00:04:18,361 --> 00:04:24,040
was pleased to see them there was tremendous debate and worry about whether it would work

47
00:04:24,041 --> 00:04:29,780
whether these tunnel boring machines would chew up all of ancient Athens as they made their way

48
00:04:29,781 --> 00:04:32,540
underneath the streets of the modern city?

49
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:36,240
Would they just be spitting out the refuse of thousands of years of culture

50
00:04:36,241 --> 00:04:40,120
as they inexorably marched toward the next breakthrough?

51
00:04:41,080 --> 00:04:45,960
The new lines would form a large cross intersecting in the heart of the modern city,

52
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:48,760
which had been built over layers of the ancient city.

53
00:04:50,180 --> 00:04:54,580
Several of the new stations were close to, or even inside, the old city wall,

54
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:57,660
one of the richest archaeological sites in the world.

55
00:04:58,580 --> 00:05:04,260
Since they've been inhabiting the same spot for thousands of years and living in exactly the same place,

56
00:05:04,540 --> 00:05:11,360
the archaeological history of Athens has built up like layer upon layer in a cake over the centuries.

57
00:05:11,780 --> 00:05:15,360
And all you need to do is take a teeny little slice in that cake,

58
00:05:15,361 --> 00:05:22,700
and you are immediately plunged into centuries, indeed millennia, of riches of archaeology.

59
00:05:22,701 --> 00:05:28,020
The careful scientific archaeological excavations carried out during the Metro project

60
00:05:28,021 --> 00:05:30,780
give us a window into the ancient civilization.

61
00:05:31,700 --> 00:05:37,520
Along with coins, lamps, and pots, water pipes and roads tell us about Greece's engineering feats

62
00:05:37,521 --> 00:05:41,080
before it was annexed by Rome in 148 B.C.

63
00:05:41,620 --> 00:05:49,240
They had roadways with drainage, with surfacing, with tunnels and pipes

64
00:05:49,241 --> 00:05:53,400
to take away wastewater and above all to bring fresh water,

65
00:05:53,920 --> 00:05:57,440
extending over an enormous amount of ground

66
00:05:57,441 --> 00:06:01,740
and bringing to the people a quality of life

67
00:06:01,741 --> 00:06:07,040
that we never had this kind of physical evidence for in previous times.

68
00:06:07,041 --> 00:06:13,380
And so we see how there really was an ancient technological marvel.

69
00:06:13,840 --> 00:06:19,120
The modern technology let us see the application of the ancient technology

70
00:06:19,121 --> 00:06:24,280
in a way that gives a completely new dimension to our understanding of ancient Athenian life.

71
00:06:25,100 --> 00:06:28,360
To build a metro in Athens at the end of the 20th century

72
00:06:28,361 --> 00:06:31,220
meant vast tracts of history had to be cut into.

73
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:35,300
The archaeologists would have to perform rescue excavations,

74
00:06:35,920 --> 00:06:40,940
working within a limited time frame to map, record, and preserve thousands of artifacts

75
00:06:40,941 --> 00:06:46,320
across 18 sites, exposing over 40 square miles of ancient civilization.

76
00:06:46,321 --> 00:06:53,320
The obviously is a very difficult marriage between the archaeologists and the engineers.

77
00:06:53,321 --> 00:06:56,320
The engineers want to do things as fast as they can.

78
00:06:56,321 --> 00:07:02,320
The archaeologists, of course, have their own time frame, which is opposite to the engineers.

79
00:07:02,321 --> 00:07:05,320
But the new subway was desperately needed.

80
00:07:05,321 --> 00:07:15,320
Stations had to be built, and the Metro Mouse cutting heads had to begin tunneling to construct the two new lines for Athens' most recent marvel.

81
00:07:16,320 --> 00:07:19,980
The ancient Parthenon took 16 years to complete.

82
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:25,700
Ox carts hauled 22,000 tons of white marble 10 miles from Mount Pantelicon.

83
00:07:26,740 --> 00:07:30,300
The Athens subway will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

84
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:41,920
At the end of the 20th century, Athens was a chaotic city with no clear urban plan and a struggling public transportation system.

85
00:07:43,540 --> 00:07:45,340
But it wasn't always this way.

86
00:07:46,320 --> 00:07:54,020
When Greece became an independent nation in the mid-19th century, tremendous efforts went into rebuilding the city while preserving her past.

87
00:07:55,520 --> 00:08:06,100
Otto I had been chosen as king, and in 1834, Athens was established as the capital city of a free Greece in honor of her historic greatness.

88
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:12,860
As the king took the throne, a model city plan for Athens was developed.

89
00:08:12,861 --> 00:08:21,200
This renaissance in Athens was part of a worldwide classical revival which became known as neoclassicism.

90
00:08:22,060 --> 00:08:27,840
One does not have to look very far to see the influence of ancient Greece.

91
00:08:28,340 --> 00:08:31,500
The founding fathers were all classical scholars.

92
00:08:32,380 --> 00:08:36,560
Neoclassicism was the first truly national architectural style in the United States.

93
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:40,920
It also inspired a revival of the Olympic Games.

94
00:08:42,860 --> 00:08:49,960
The first modern Olympiad took place in Greece in 1896, setting in motion the 20th century

95
00:08:49,961 --> 00:08:52,801
phenomena of the modern Olympics.

96
00:08:52,802 --> 00:08:58,140
Line 1 serviced these early games and continued to grow north,

97
00:08:58,141 --> 00:09:00,420
eventually reaching the suburb of Kyphysia.

98
00:09:00,421 --> 00:09:07,940
The history of Line 1 starts a number of years after the Greek Revolution, when the

99
00:09:07,941 --> 00:09:14,180
port city of Piraeus needed to be connected with Athens.

100
00:09:14,181 --> 00:09:19,480
The first stage of Line 1 was constructed on or just below the surface.

101
00:09:19,481 --> 00:09:26,120
It was one of the earliest urban transport systems in the world when it opened in 1869.

102
00:09:26,121 --> 00:09:32,340
When the lines first started running, it was running on a single track with steam engines.

103
00:09:32,341 --> 00:09:37,420
Slowly, the line expanded further north.

104
00:09:37,421 --> 00:09:42,660
As it grew into the city, they used the time-honored cut and cover technique also used in Paris

105
00:09:42,661 --> 00:09:44,200
and New York.

106
00:09:44,201 --> 00:09:49,300
First, a large hole is excavated, supporting walls are built.

107
00:09:49,301 --> 00:09:55,300
Then a roof is constructed before reinstating surface conditions.

108
00:09:55,301 --> 00:09:59,580
Towards the end of the 19th century, more and more people traveled to Athens to experience

109
00:09:59,581 --> 00:10:05,040
her classical glory.

110
00:10:05,041 --> 00:10:12,580
But the events of the 20th century overtook Athens' development.

111
00:10:12,581 --> 00:10:19,520
She was involved in both world wars and suffered under Italian and German occupation.

112
00:10:19,521 --> 00:10:25,700
She then endured a bitter civil war from 1946 to 1949, in which more than one million Greeks

113
00:10:25,701 --> 00:10:31,320
were killed.

114
00:10:31,321 --> 00:10:38,320
Living in rural areas led to a mass migration into Athens, causing social and economic chaos.

115
00:10:41,320 --> 00:10:45,320
The explosion of the development started after the Second World War.

116
00:10:45,321 --> 00:10:56,320
The government did apply many regulations and they just let people to build high buildings in the center and that created many problems.

117
00:10:56,321 --> 00:11:03,899
problems. This caused an unplanned dense sprawl of poorly made housing.

118
00:11:03,900 --> 00:11:05,820
Late into the 20th

119
00:11:05,821 --> 00:11:11,260
century, Greece continued to struggle for stability, until democracy was restored in

120
00:11:11,261 --> 00:11:16,877
1974. Today, Greece is a parliamentary republic.

121
00:11:16,878 --> 00:11:22,260
The crowded city, a result of its recent past,

122
00:11:22,261 --> 00:11:25,460
further complicated the engineering of the new metro project.

123
00:11:27,840 --> 00:11:32,820
Lines 2 and 3 would radiate out in four directions from Syntagma Square,

124
00:11:33,480 --> 00:11:35,480
connecting the suburb with downtown Athens.

125
00:11:36,520 --> 00:11:40,820
It was impossible to open up huge cut-and-cover trenches in the middle of the city.

126
00:11:42,680 --> 00:11:45,960
Instead, the existing infrastructure forced Attico Metro

127
00:11:45,961 --> 00:11:49,200
to dig some of the central stations from the bottom up.

128
00:11:49,201 --> 00:11:56,920
A small service hole was dug, through which the giant station cavities were excavated under the bustling city.

129
00:11:59,360 --> 00:12:03,580
At interchange stations with Line 1, like Ammonia and Monasteraki,

130
00:12:04,120 --> 00:12:08,080
the old stations had to remain open and in operation throughout construction,

131
00:12:08,780 --> 00:12:10,940
creating even more of a challenge for the engineers.

132
00:12:13,460 --> 00:12:18,880
Outside of the city center, however, most of the new stations could be constructed in the same way as Line 1,

133
00:12:19,200 --> 00:12:21,740
using a modern version of the cut and cover technique.

134
00:12:23,060 --> 00:12:24,840
An open trench is excavated.

135
00:12:26,040 --> 00:12:29,560
The sides are retained by strutted or reinforced concrete pile walls.

136
00:12:30,400 --> 00:12:32,140
A waterproof membrane is then laid.

137
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:33,560
Formwork follows.

138
00:12:34,220 --> 00:12:38,260
Finally, a slab roof is put in place, and the area above is backfilled.

139
00:12:39,620 --> 00:12:41,740
These stations then had to be joined by tunnels.

140
00:12:43,340 --> 00:12:46,600
Two huge tunnel boring machines, or TBMs,

141
00:12:46,740 --> 00:12:48,820
specially designed by Mitsubishi in Japan,

142
00:12:49,200 --> 00:12:52,260
have been manufactured in France and were assembled on site.

143
00:12:53,880 --> 00:12:57,760
Each TBM was fitted with a 13-foot diameter cutting head,

144
00:12:58,240 --> 00:13:02,020
made up of eight spokes with drag bits and single disc cutters.

145
00:13:03,340 --> 00:13:08,300
Costing over $20 million, each machine weighs 1,650 tons

146
00:13:08,301 --> 00:13:11,400
and is 459 feet in length.

147
00:13:12,740 --> 00:13:17,360
TBMs can excavate at depths of 20 to 92 feet below the surface.

148
00:13:17,361 --> 00:13:21,200
Deciding the depths of tunnels under Athens was crucial.

149
00:13:22,200 --> 00:13:28,460
In building a metro, you always want to make it as shallow as you can be and be in stable grounds.

150
00:13:29,120 --> 00:13:38,300
Because, of course, the shallower the metro, the cheaper it is to have escalators and elevators and stairways.

151
00:13:39,100 --> 00:13:44,100
But archaeologists were worried that tunneling at shallow depths could destroy historical evidence.

152
00:13:44,101 --> 00:13:54,100
Antiquities in Athens can occur anywhere between 20 centimeters below the surface of the ground,

153
00:13:54,101 --> 00:14:00,100
up to 15 or even 20 meters where the bedrock begins.

154
00:14:00,101 --> 00:14:08,100
To avoid buried antiquities, it was decided to operate the cutting heads under Athens at a depth of 66 to 82 feet.

155
00:14:08,101 --> 00:14:18,980
The TBM heads can rotate at a speed of 4 RPM and can cut up to 33 feet per day, leaving behind a 30-foot diameter tunnel,

156
00:14:19,420 --> 00:14:27,240
which is then supported by a ring of precast reinforced concrete segments, secured with a key segment to form a 28-foot lined tunnel.

157
00:14:28,940 --> 00:14:34,420
But the huge TBMs vibrate as they cut through solid rock and have been known to cause subsidence.

158
00:14:34,421 --> 00:14:40,660
Subtidence or settlement in building any metro tunnel or even water tunnel, for example,

159
00:14:40,661 --> 00:14:46,420
or sewer tunnel is normal, as long as it kept to a minimum and is monitored very carefully.

160
00:14:46,421 --> 00:14:51,220
Archaeologists feared that vibrations could damage the ancient buildings

161
00:14:51,780 --> 00:14:55,540
as the TBMs approached the stations at the heart of the Old City.

162
00:14:55,541 --> 00:15:06,780
The deepest underground system in the world is thought to be the Pyongyang Metro in North Korea.

163
00:15:07,600 --> 00:15:11,460
Tunnels are as deep as 393 feet below the surface.

164
00:15:12,280 --> 00:15:16,460
The Athens subway will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

165
00:15:16,461 --> 00:15:27,740
The Athens Metro is one of the biggest engineering feats in Europe, and the largest construction

166
00:15:27,741 --> 00:15:29,820
project in Greece.

167
00:15:29,821 --> 00:15:35,500
By 1995, as the frames of the stations neared completion, giant TBMs began cutting through

168
00:15:35,501 --> 00:15:40,620
solid rock to join them together, tunneling under ancient Athens, one of the oldest sites

169
00:15:40,621 --> 00:15:42,900
of civilization in the world.

170
00:15:42,901 --> 00:15:46,580
The TBM is the preferred method of tunneling if you can use it.

171
00:15:46,860 --> 00:15:51,660
So where you know that you have consistent ground for long distances.

172
00:15:52,660 --> 00:15:57,300
The TBMs are computer-operated, electrically powered, and hydraulically driven.

173
00:15:58,820 --> 00:16:04,780
Up to 250 men work 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, to man them in three shifts,

174
00:16:05,240 --> 00:16:07,720
contending with incredibly hot and humid conditions.

175
00:16:07,721 --> 00:16:12,840
work only stops when the temperature rises above 100 degrees Fahrenheit.

176
00:16:14,840 --> 00:16:19,560
As the cutters break up the soil and rock, the debris is removed via conveyor belts,

177
00:16:21,080 --> 00:16:22,660
automatically loaded onto trains,

178
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:27,800
and transported through tunnels to points where it is raised to the surface

179
00:16:27,801 --> 00:16:29,860
and taken away for disposal.

180
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:35,840
As TBMs drive through Athens' substratum towards already prepared station sites,

181
00:16:35,841 --> 00:16:38,240
their arrival generates much excitement.

182
00:16:45,760 --> 00:16:49,640
Each time a TBM breaks into a station with pinpoint accuracy,

183
00:16:50,240 --> 00:16:52,300
there are celebrations at its safe arrival.

184
00:17:05,840 --> 00:17:10,020
But the extraordinary geological conditions under Athens are very unpredictable.

185
00:17:11,800 --> 00:17:15,820
Despite surveys and hundreds of boreholes, in a space of 33 feet,

186
00:17:15,821 --> 00:17:20,480
the geology can change from hard rock to soft rock or even shale.

187
00:17:22,080 --> 00:17:26,820
Because of this inconsistency, tunneling methods had to be adapted to suit conditions.

188
00:17:28,020 --> 00:17:33,540
For a section of line two, another machine specially designed to deal with softer rock was brought in.

189
00:17:33,541 --> 00:17:40,380
Called an open-face shield TBM, it works like a tunnel boring machine, but has much smaller

190
00:17:40,381 --> 00:17:44,820
cutting heads attached to it, and a shield to provide initial ground support when tunneling.

191
00:17:44,821 --> 00:17:53,920
The open-face shield machine is a more gentle and more secure method of tunneling, especially

192
00:17:53,921 --> 00:18:01,100
in a weaker ground and where you have tunneling going under weak and sensitive buildings.

193
00:18:03,540 --> 00:18:07,960
Thousands of years of urban development added to the geological instability.

194
00:18:08,860 --> 00:18:14,980
In areas like Panopistimio, landfills and ancient water wells litter the naturally unstable subsoil.

195
00:18:17,800 --> 00:18:23,960
When the water vanished from the well, the well had to be filled up.

196
00:18:25,060 --> 00:18:32,920
Usually it was filled up with rubbish, with broken pots that were useless for the household.

197
00:18:33,540 --> 00:18:40,540
Sometimes when a well was cut into, artifacts cascaded into the tunnel.

198
00:18:40,541 --> 00:18:49,540
If and when you discover something, then the involvement of the archaeologists become really the driving force, not the engineers.

199
00:18:49,541 --> 00:18:52,540
This ancient debris is a treasure trove.

200
00:18:52,541 --> 00:19:01,540
All that became a very important archaeological find which gives us an insight of everyday life.

201
00:19:01,541 --> 00:19:09,660
because ancient Greeks, like modern Greeks, wanted the new.

202
00:19:10,180 --> 00:19:11,520
They wanted the latest.

203
00:19:11,521 --> 00:19:15,660
They did not want to use last year's pottery.

204
00:19:15,960 --> 00:19:18,480
They wanted to use this year's pottery.

205
00:19:19,280 --> 00:19:25,900
The decoration, the shape, the style of the pottery was continuously changing.

206
00:19:25,901 --> 00:19:33,720
You can work out the chronological development of these pottery styles.

207
00:19:37,300 --> 00:19:39,860
When the archaeologists had finished their work,

208
00:19:40,220 --> 00:19:42,760
the engineers moved back in to repair the collapse

209
00:19:42,761 --> 00:19:44,720
and minimize any further settlement

210
00:19:44,721 --> 00:19:47,260
by pumping concrete deep underground,

211
00:19:47,720 --> 00:19:49,680
directly in front of the TBM's cutting head.

212
00:19:49,681 --> 00:19:59,080
But in some areas, the threat of subsidence from the TBM and the open-faced tunnel boring

213
00:19:59,081 --> 00:20:06,180
machines was considered too great a risk for the ancient buildings above.

214
00:20:06,181 --> 00:20:10,440
Because of the presence of many beautifully preserved early Christian churches, the tunnel

215
00:20:10,441 --> 00:20:16,120
connecting Syntagma Station to Monasteraki Station inside the city wall, and then on

216
00:20:16,121 --> 00:20:18,320
to Kerimikos proved the most difficult.

217
00:20:20,160 --> 00:20:22,620
The route of this tunnel had already been changed

218
00:20:22,621 --> 00:20:24,820
because it went under the Greek Orthodox Cathedral.

219
00:20:26,820 --> 00:20:30,340
But the new rerouting then meant the TBM would have to cut under

220
00:20:30,341 --> 00:20:34,380
the Byzantine Church of the Kapna Korea, built in the 11th century AD.

221
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:40,280
There it stands in the middle of Athens as a concrete symbol

222
00:20:40,281 --> 00:20:44,960
of a thousand years of Christian faith in Greece.

223
00:20:44,961 --> 00:20:51,960
amidst the bustle and congestion and high-rise buildings of modern Athens.

224
00:20:52,480 --> 00:21:00,840
There, it's a quiet oasis whose art and architecture speak to the long-term continuity of Christianity

225
00:21:00,841 --> 00:21:04,400
as such an important part of Greek culture.

226
00:21:04,920 --> 00:21:10,600
For this section of tunneling, the engineers resorted to a technique called the New Austrian Tunneling Method.

227
00:21:10,601 --> 00:21:17,420
Developed in the 1960s, this method of tunneling provided a much more controlled environment

228
00:21:17,421 --> 00:21:22,560
for tackling particularly difficult ground conditions.

229
00:21:22,561 --> 00:21:27,060
The tunnel is constructed by slowly digging out segments of the hole diameter.

230
00:21:27,061 --> 00:21:34,360
Each segment is then supported with steel arches and reinforced with wire mesh, sprayed

231
00:21:34,361 --> 00:21:38,880
with shot concrete and further strengthened with rock bolts.

232
00:21:38,881 --> 00:21:43,280
The lining becomes an integrated part of the rock mass and limits subsidence to the highest

233
00:21:43,281 --> 00:21:46,620
possible degree.

234
00:21:46,621 --> 00:21:50,580
Until the side segments are complete, the center of the tunnel is left alone in order

235
00:21:50,581 --> 00:21:54,480
to provide extra support protecting the buildings above.

236
00:21:54,481 --> 00:22:01,320
This is then dug away to create an oval tunnel.

237
00:22:01,321 --> 00:22:05,980
Using the new Austrian method had prevented any major surface damage along this route.

238
00:22:05,981 --> 00:22:13,980
However, progress is reduced to 10 to 16 feet of tunnel per day, whereas TBMs can dig up to 33 feet per day.

239
00:22:17,980 --> 00:22:26,457
But as soon as the digging began at the Monastiraki Station, one of the central station sites being dug from the bottom up, unacceptable levels of subsidence were

240
00:22:26,458 --> 00:22:27,158
occurring.

241
00:22:27,159 --> 00:22:37,400
Here in the middle of this difficult building site, the 17th century church of the Pantanasa

242
00:22:37,401 --> 00:22:40,780
had already suffered extensive structural damage from settlement.

243
00:22:43,060 --> 00:22:47,020
Right next to the Agora, the ancient marketplace in classical Athens,

244
00:22:47,580 --> 00:22:51,720
the area around the Monasteraki station is still a cramped, bustling market.

245
00:22:52,560 --> 00:22:56,220
The old Line 1 station had been built here over a hundred years ago.

246
00:22:56,980 --> 00:23:01,980
and is one of the interchange stations between Line 1 and the new Line 3.

247
00:23:01,981 --> 00:23:08,980
The old station had to remain open and trains in operation throughout construction.

248
00:23:08,981 --> 00:23:14,740
Along with difficult geological conditions, the presence of springs and old wells at the site

249
00:23:14,741 --> 00:23:17,780
further added to the problems of the engineers.

250
00:23:17,781 --> 00:23:22,480
The station Mostraki represented probably the greatest challenge for all of us.

251
00:23:22,481 --> 00:23:33,200
We actually divided the excretion method into very small steps to ensure that every step is taken with the proper protection.

252
00:23:33,940 --> 00:23:39,540
Therefore, the settlement which we monitor two or three times a day will be kept at the absolute minimum.

253
00:23:41,020 --> 00:23:46,260
To finish digging the station, the engineers had to resort to more ingenious ways of tunneling.

254
00:23:46,261 --> 00:23:53,260
International consultants recommended state-of-the-art microtunnel boring machines.

255
00:23:53,261 --> 00:23:58,260
They operate like ordinary TBMs, but are significantly smaller.

256
00:23:58,261 --> 00:24:04,260
Here they were used to cut an arch of small tunnels, which was then reinforced with steel tubes.

257
00:24:04,261 --> 00:24:12,260
This umbrella of tubes was then bolted together and filled with concrete to secure the surface,

258
00:24:12,261 --> 00:24:15,260
before excavating the station area beneath.

259
00:24:15,261 --> 00:24:22,500
Despite using micro-TBM technology, there was still enough settlement here to force engineers

260
00:24:22,501 --> 00:24:26,380
to reduce the size of the station.

261
00:24:26,381 --> 00:24:31,500
The archaeologists had grave concerns about tunneling in any form under ancient Athens.

262
00:24:31,501 --> 00:24:34,500
One tunnel even caused an international outcry.

263
00:24:34,501 --> 00:24:39,220
To connect Monasteraki to Kerimikos Station meant digging a tunnel under the sacred gate

264
00:24:39,221 --> 00:24:45,040
of the Old City Wall and under the ancient graveyards of Kerimikos.

265
00:24:45,041 --> 00:24:50,280
were alarmed that vibrations could crack and damage irreplaceable antiquities.

266
00:24:52,820 --> 00:25:00,020
With 468 stations, the MTA New York City Transit has the most underground stations in the world.

267
00:25:00,780 --> 00:25:03,520
The Athens subway will continue on Modern Marvels.

268
00:25:09,520 --> 00:25:14,240
Under modern Athens, engineers struggled with a variety of tunneling methods

269
00:25:14,241 --> 00:25:16,680
to cut through the unpredictable substratum

270
00:25:16,681 --> 00:25:20,640
in building the new lines 2 and 3 of the modern metro system.

271
00:25:22,040 --> 00:25:26,300
Alongside them, archaeologists struggled to conduct their work.

272
00:25:27,280 --> 00:25:31,680
The Ministry of Culture, working hand-in-hand with the engineers of Attico Metro,

273
00:25:32,340 --> 00:25:35,320
had identified key areas of archaeological interest.

274
00:25:36,680 --> 00:25:40,540
The areas like Acropolis and Syntagma,

275
00:25:40,541 --> 00:25:44,240
what you have to do before you even start the construction,

276
00:25:45,020 --> 00:25:49,040
that the archaeologists do what you call archaeological trenches,

277
00:25:49,800 --> 00:25:52,000
which is very, very carefully, by hand,

278
00:25:52,420 --> 00:25:55,240
you expose the surface layer by layer.

279
00:25:55,241 --> 00:25:57,640
The Athens Metro excavations are spectacular

280
00:25:57,641 --> 00:26:01,840
because they pitted the largest modern mechanical tools

281
00:26:01,841 --> 00:26:05,100
against the smallest archaeological implements,

282
00:26:05,101 --> 00:26:10,960
tunnel boring machines against the little brushes and picks of the archaeologists.

283
00:26:12,080 --> 00:26:17,440
They would have to conduct their painstaking work within the engineer's much shorter time frame.

284
00:26:20,660 --> 00:26:26,820
The rescue digs involved more than 50 archaeologists who recorded and researched the areas,

285
00:26:27,480 --> 00:26:31,120
assisted by as many as 200 laborers who worked around the clock.

286
00:26:31,121 --> 00:26:40,100
In total, 18 sites were excavated, exposing over 213,000 feet of the center of one of

287
00:26:40,101 --> 00:26:43,850
the oldest sites in Western civilization.

288
00:26:43,851 --> 00:26:48,240
It represents the largest archaeological program

289
00:26:48,241 --> 00:26:50,160
ever undertaken in Athens.

290
00:26:50,161 --> 00:26:58,160
More than 30,000 removable finds were produced during the project, which cover a time span

291
00:26:58,161 --> 00:27:05,160
of 25 centuries from the 17th century BC to the 8th century AD.

292
00:27:06,160 --> 00:27:10,160
Perhaps the most outstanding artifact found in the metro excavations

293
00:27:10,161 --> 00:27:14,160
was discovered almost entirely encased in rock.

294
00:27:14,161 --> 00:27:19,160
When carefully chipped away, it revealed this beautifully crafted bronze head

295
00:27:19,161 --> 00:27:23,160
made in classical Greece in about 480 BC.

296
00:27:23,161 --> 00:27:28,900
Very few large bronzes survive from antiquity, even fewer from Greece.

297
00:27:29,780 --> 00:27:33,780
It's because bronze was a reusable material, also an expensive one.

298
00:27:33,781 --> 00:27:40,740
It tells us a tremendous amount about what public monuments might have looked like in ancient Athens.

299
00:27:40,940 --> 00:27:45,640
But he's still got his eyelashes. He's got inlaid copper eyebrows.

300
00:27:46,260 --> 00:27:51,020
Inlaid eyes survive. It's an unparalleled piece.

301
00:27:51,021 --> 00:27:56,120
But the vast majority of the artifacts were more ordinary domestic objects.

302
00:27:57,520 --> 00:28:03,880
More than 500 movable finds were displayed in an exhibition in Athens called The City Beneath the City.

303
00:28:05,100 --> 00:28:12,800
They are fractions of everyday life, and they show items that these people used at home,

304
00:28:13,140 --> 00:28:19,880
in the playground, school, in political, religious life, or took with them after death.

305
00:28:21,020 --> 00:28:27,720
Several of the central station sites were situated on ancient burial grounds just outside the old city wall,

306
00:28:27,960 --> 00:28:30,480
where thousands of new graves were uncovered.

307
00:28:31,440 --> 00:28:40,220
Ancient Greeks throughout their history always wanted to keep dead away from the inhabited areas.

308
00:28:40,840 --> 00:28:45,280
When they had walls, the dead were buried outside the walls.

309
00:28:45,281 --> 00:28:48,880
The ancient Greeks generally made tombs for their dead,

310
00:28:49,320 --> 00:28:52,320
and stone coffins often preserved their contents intact.

311
00:28:53,280 --> 00:28:56,680
In classical times, various religious and civic observances

312
00:28:56,681 --> 00:28:58,480
were practiced around burial rites.

313
00:28:59,240 --> 00:29:03,320
The Greeks buried the dead with a number of burial gifts

314
00:29:03,321 --> 00:29:08,880
which were meant to suggest who the deceased was in their lifetime.

315
00:29:09,760 --> 00:29:12,380
So soldiers are buried with their weaponry,

316
00:29:12,381 --> 00:29:16,300
athletes are buried with their athletic paraphernalia,

317
00:29:16,820 --> 00:29:22,460
wives are buried with household items, and so on and so forth.

318
00:29:24,020 --> 00:29:30,340
Every Athenian was in death the person who were in their lifetime.

319
00:29:31,700 --> 00:29:36,680
Children who had died were buried with their favorite toys, like these clay figures.

320
00:29:36,681 --> 00:29:47,060
babies with their feeder cups or tiny pots containing food for them to take into the afterlife.

321
00:29:51,840 --> 00:29:58,340
The oldest exhibit in this exhibition is a pair of pots from the Mycenaean period

322
00:29:58,341 --> 00:30:00,400
dating in about the 17th century BC.

323
00:30:01,120 --> 00:30:05,760
They come from the grave of a child in the area of the Acropolis Station.

324
00:30:05,761 --> 00:30:09,180
This child's burial is unique for two reasons.

325
00:30:09,520 --> 00:30:14,900
One, that it belongs to a period of the Bronze Age civilization,

326
00:30:15,220 --> 00:30:19,880
which is not well documented, the transition from the Middle Bronze to the Late Bronze

327
00:30:19,881 --> 00:30:21,680
or to the beginning of the Mycenaean era.

328
00:30:22,280 --> 00:30:30,080
And secondly, that the vases themselves are shapes that we normally associate with mature adults.

329
00:30:30,420 --> 00:30:35,060
At the Kirameko Station site alone, over a thousand new tombs were found.

330
00:30:35,760 --> 00:30:40,180
Some graves had more unusual contents, like this one from Mann's best friend.

331
00:30:40,980 --> 00:30:43,060
Pet cemeteries are not a modern invention.

332
00:30:43,061 --> 00:30:49,320
This beloved pet was laid out there with his leather collar studded with bronze discs

333
00:30:49,321 --> 00:30:54,580
and actual glass perfume bottles in with him,

334
00:30:55,080 --> 00:31:02,020
which is surely an expression of the close bond between the family and this beloved pet.

335
00:31:02,021 --> 00:31:07,020
As the rescue digs came to an end, the bulldozers moved in.

336
00:31:07,021 --> 00:31:10,020
New stations and tunnels needed to be built.

337
00:31:10,021 --> 00:31:16,020
The most controversial stretch of tunneling to connect Monasteraki Station to Kerimikos Station

338
00:31:16,021 --> 00:31:20,020
was to be cut under the ancient suburb of Kerimikos.

339
00:31:20,021 --> 00:31:25,020
In the ancient world, Kerimikos was described as one of the most beautiful districts of Athens.

340
00:31:25,021 --> 00:31:30,020
It was the potter's quarter, the place from which we get the word, ceramic.

341
00:31:30,021 --> 00:31:35,020
Located just outside the old city wall in the shadow of the Acropolis, it is the

342
00:31:35,021 --> 00:31:40,420
site of the largest and most ancient cemetery yet to be found on Attica. It

343
00:31:40,421 --> 00:31:44,620
also bears testimony to one of the most tragic episodes in ancient Greece, a

344
00:31:44,621 --> 00:31:50,020
plague that devastated Athens in 430 BC during her war with Sparta.

345
00:31:50,021 --> 00:31:54,520
The great war that Athens fought with Sparta brought them behind their walls

346
00:31:54,521 --> 00:32:02,520
for defense and a great epidemic struck them. This disease killed thousands and

347
00:32:02,521 --> 00:32:08,980
as the excavation shows led the Athenians to pile the corpses one on top

348
00:32:08,981 --> 00:32:14,860
of each other in these communal graves giving up their traditions of individual

349
00:32:14,861 --> 00:32:20,440
burial because the tragedy was so overwhelming. The Greeks did not usually

350
00:32:20,441 --> 00:32:25,400
usually practice mass burial, but during the plague, wrote the Greek historian Thucydides,

351
00:32:25,401 --> 00:32:29,100
the Athenians were so sure that the end of the world was coming that they left their

352
00:32:29,101 --> 00:32:31,800
dead in the streets.

353
00:32:31,801 --> 00:32:36,880
Potentially, through DNA analysis, we might be able to settle some questions regarding

354
00:32:36,881 --> 00:32:38,822
the nature of this plague itself.

355
00:32:38,823 --> 00:32:41,940
What was the disease that carried off these citizens?

356
00:32:41,941 --> 00:32:44,432
We have descriptions of the symptoms in Thucydides.

357
00:32:44,433 --> 00:32:46,680
There has been a lot of speculation about what

358
00:32:46,681 --> 00:32:51,720
the actual disease was, but since we now actually have the bones of citizens that died as a

359
00:32:51,721 --> 00:32:55,620
result of this plague, we might in fact be able to pinpoint what precisely the nature

360
00:32:55,621 --> 00:32:56,321
of it was.

361
00:32:57,340 --> 00:33:02,420
Originally, a TBM was to have cut beneath the ancient fortifications, under the bed of the

362
00:33:02,421 --> 00:33:08,140
Eridonis River, which in classical times ran out of the city beside the sacred gate and

363
00:33:08,141 --> 00:33:09,760
on under the ancient graveyards.

364
00:33:10,600 --> 00:33:15,420
This plan caused a storm of opposition from archaeologists, who were concerned that the

365
00:33:15,421 --> 00:33:17,980
tunneling could damage irreplaceable antiquities.

366
00:33:19,900 --> 00:33:28,780
As a concession, and to ensure that there is not even slight possibility of any damage to the ancient cemetery,

367
00:33:28,781 --> 00:33:36,240
we agreed with the archaeologists to proceed using these new Austrian tunneling methods,

368
00:33:36,600 --> 00:33:40,180
which is a much more careful and gentle method of tunneling.

369
00:33:41,000 --> 00:33:44,420
But still, the archaeologists were not satisfied.

370
00:33:45,220 --> 00:33:47,920
Attico Metro finally agreed to reroute the tunnel.

371
00:33:48,720 --> 00:33:50,560
But to connect to Kerimikos Station,

372
00:33:50,860 --> 00:33:52,280
which was already under construction,

373
00:33:52,960 --> 00:33:55,540
they would still have to cut under a corner of the cemetery.

374
00:33:57,700 --> 00:34:00,800
And that corner was below the Street of Tombs,

375
00:34:01,220 --> 00:34:02,560
in the Kerimikos neighborhood

376
00:34:02,561 --> 00:34:05,700
containing important pieces of classical funerary art.

377
00:34:06,700 --> 00:34:08,040
The ancient sources tell us

378
00:34:08,041 --> 00:34:10,460
it was the most beautiful suburb of Athens.

379
00:34:10,461 --> 00:34:16,520
It was also the place where Athens commemorated its war dead,

380
00:34:16,620 --> 00:34:21,300
where the National Memorial of the Fallen Soldiers was located,

381
00:34:21,760 --> 00:34:25,980
and where many tombs with beautiful works of art were placed.

382
00:34:26,360 --> 00:34:29,000
It was a sacred place of commemoration.

383
00:34:29,820 --> 00:34:33,220
The Greek, French, American, and German archaeologists

384
00:34:33,221 --> 00:34:36,080
joined hands to oppose the tunnel under the cemetery.

385
00:34:36,400 --> 00:34:38,560
The European Union stepped in,

386
00:34:38,561 --> 00:34:43,980
And by April 1998, the Greek government called a halt to tunneling under the cemetery.

387
00:34:46,320 --> 00:34:48,860
The Karameko station was abandoned.

388
00:34:50,740 --> 00:34:58,160
In planning any project in an ancient city, you have to plan considering the impact of the archaeology.

389
00:34:58,660 --> 00:35:03,080
So we added 12 months to schedule to allow for such activity.

390
00:35:03,081 --> 00:35:12,460
But Attico Metro now believes that the archaeological digs delayed the project by up to two years, at a cost of nearly $50 million.

391
00:35:13,740 --> 00:35:21,560
Behind schedule and plagued by delays, the engineers struggled to complete the base project in time for the new millennium.

392
00:35:23,580 --> 00:35:29,460
At 33.5 miles long, the Seikan Tunnel in Japan is the world's longest railway tunnel.

393
00:35:29,461 --> 00:35:34,220
The Athens subway will return on Modern Marvels.

394
00:35:40,480 --> 00:35:42,880
After more than seven years of construction,

395
00:35:43,540 --> 00:35:46,700
excavating 12 miles of tunnels and 20 new stations,

396
00:35:47,540 --> 00:35:50,840
alongside the largest archaeological rescue dig in Athens,

397
00:35:51,420 --> 00:35:53,460
the Athens Metro was nearly ready.

398
00:35:54,900 --> 00:35:58,180
Blessed by the Archbishop and opened by the President and the Prime Minister,

399
00:35:58,181 --> 00:36:04,140
the first stage of Athens' new metro was finally open on January 29, 2000.

400
00:36:06,640 --> 00:36:09,620
Throughout the nearly $2.5 billion project,

401
00:36:10,280 --> 00:36:12,320
over 2 million cubic feet of concrete

402
00:36:12,321 --> 00:36:17,320
and 62.5 tons of structural and reinforcing steel were used.

403
00:36:18,780 --> 00:36:21,360
One of the disadvantages of building metros,

404
00:36:22,080 --> 00:36:23,500
that underground especially,

405
00:36:23,860 --> 00:36:26,440
that people do not see any development in the surface.

406
00:36:26,441 --> 00:36:32,140
So when we finally opened, every Greek came to celebrate the communists.

407
00:36:34,580 --> 00:36:40,700
Tickets were free for the first weekend, and over 900,000 people used the metro over those two days.

408
00:36:41,400 --> 00:36:43,920
That's one in ten of the population of Greece.

409
00:36:46,820 --> 00:36:52,880
The decision was made to incorporate finds from the excavations into the designs of the stations at that site.

410
00:36:56,440 --> 00:37:10,440
The idea to present some of the exhibits, either in the original or in copies in reproduction, in the stations, was very welcomed by the people who used the metro.

411
00:37:15,280 --> 00:37:25,420
As people say that we don't have a museum within a metro, but a metro within a museum.

412
00:37:25,421 --> 00:37:31,920
These are, in a sense, the most successful public outreach programs because they brought

413
00:37:31,921 --> 00:37:34,920
the culture, these museums, to the people.

414
00:37:34,921 --> 00:37:42,420
And it gives a sense of continuity to reassure the Athenian but also the international public

415
00:37:42,421 --> 00:37:48,420
that not damage was suffered to the remains of the past during the project.

416
00:37:48,421 --> 00:37:55,180
More than 400,000 people use the new Metro daily, effortlessly transporting them around

417
00:37:55,181 --> 00:38:01,180
the center and out into the suburbs.

418
00:38:01,181 --> 00:38:06,500
Trains run for 18 hours a day between 6 a.m. and 12 p.m.

419
00:38:06,501 --> 00:38:11,420
In rush hour, trains run every three minutes, and every five to ten minutes in non-rush

420
00:38:11,421 --> 00:38:14,820
hour.

421
00:38:14,821 --> 00:38:19,500
As people change lines by walking along these impressive marble corridors, they can enjoy

422
00:38:19,501 --> 00:38:22,380
not only ancient artifacts, but modern art too.

423
00:38:27,400 --> 00:38:30,540
The new metro system is not the biggest or the deepest,

424
00:38:30,900 --> 00:38:33,220
but mile for mile, it's very effective.

425
00:38:34,320 --> 00:38:37,840
It will benefit the city and the people of Athens in many ways.

426
00:38:39,740 --> 00:38:42,780
140 million tickets were sold in its first year.

427
00:38:45,520 --> 00:38:48,380
And as more and more people leave their cars behind,

428
00:38:48,381 --> 00:38:51,240
or at the specially designed park and ride stations,

429
00:38:51,840 --> 00:38:56,280
it will significantly reduce toxic emissions in the city and improve air quality.

430
00:38:57,480 --> 00:39:01,120
Now that there's a metro, the air has cleared.

431
00:39:01,440 --> 00:39:04,280
You can see all the way to the sea at the Piraeus.

432
00:39:05,060 --> 00:39:07,580
You can see the Acropolis all the time.

433
00:39:07,581 --> 00:39:14,900
We have documented that the metro has reduced the pollutant in the environment by as much as 15%.

434
00:39:14,901 --> 00:39:20,160
the number of pollutants will be decreased further as the metro expands to the out suburbs.

435
00:39:21,280 --> 00:39:24,200
But even this extended metro is only the beginning.

436
00:39:24,900 --> 00:39:28,280
It will not solve all of Athens' pollution and congestion problems.

437
00:39:29,280 --> 00:39:33,180
It is very important to take measures in the surface of the city.

438
00:39:34,200 --> 00:39:39,500
And these kind of measures are, for example, the creation of a network of tram,

439
00:39:40,380 --> 00:39:43,840
because tram takes room from the car.

440
00:39:44,900 --> 00:39:52,120
As do other forms of surface transport like buses, Athens already has the largest fleet of gas-powered buses in Europe.

441
00:39:55,320 --> 00:40:03,440
For the 2004 Olympics, Athens linked archaeological sites through leafy pedestrian routes between the ancient marvels at its center.

442
00:40:05,580 --> 00:40:11,120
It is partly because of the metro that the ancient spectacle of the Olympics returned to Greece.

443
00:40:11,121 --> 00:40:21,120
The base project was already 60% under construction when it was included in the initial Olympic bid and helped Athens secure the 2004 Games.

444
00:40:21,121 --> 00:40:25,120
The extended metro was at the heart of the Olympic master plan.

445
00:40:25,121 --> 00:40:32,120
The 2004 Olympics pushed through the completion of a new airport and direct road and rail links with Central Europe.

446
00:40:32,121 --> 00:40:33,729
with Central Europe.

447
00:40:33,730 --> 00:40:39,120
The Athens Metro project is currently working on its first phase of

448
00:40:39,121 --> 00:40:46,120
extensions, which will be gradually rolled out between 2004 and 2007. The extensions

449
00:40:46,121 --> 00:40:51,035
include nearly nine miles of underground line and 12 new stations.

450
00:40:51,036 --> 00:40:53,120
But undoubtedly the greatest

451
00:40:53,121 --> 00:40:59,120
achievement of the Metro has been to enable a unique exploration into Athens' archaeological

452
00:40:59,121 --> 00:40:59,844
heritage.

453
00:40:59,845 --> 00:41:06,920
Although the project for the construction of the Metropolitan Railway was a great, great

454
00:41:06,921 --> 00:41:13,560
challenge for the archaeologists working in Athens, it also provided a very rare opportunity.

455
00:41:14,180 --> 00:41:19,580
I don't think anyone would have agreed for a proper excavation in the heart of Athens

456
00:41:19,581 --> 00:41:25,580
in Syndagma Square, for example, if it wasn't for something like the metro project.

457
00:41:25,581 --> 00:41:39,581
In a modern city where facilities, working facilities for the general public have to be done, there is always a balance between the needs of the public and the

458
00:41:42,482 --> 00:41:45,140
scientific investigation.

459
00:41:46,420 --> 00:41:54,360
This marriage between progress and preservation has led to the discovery of thousands of everyday artifacts of extraordinary value.

460
00:41:55,160 --> 00:41:58,560
scientifically preserving the lives of everyday people of the past.

461
00:41:59,560 --> 00:42:03,680
If there hadn't been these TBMs, tunnel boring machines,

462
00:42:04,080 --> 00:42:05,580
then there wouldn't have been any subway.

463
00:42:06,000 --> 00:42:07,260
If there hadn't been any subway,

464
00:42:07,700 --> 00:42:11,080
there wouldn't have been this massive archaeological project,

465
00:42:11,460 --> 00:42:14,300
the largest ever in Athenian archaeology.

466
00:42:14,500 --> 00:42:17,500
And if there hadn't been this massive archaeological project,

467
00:42:17,501 --> 00:42:20,800
then there wouldn't have been this flowering of knowledge

468
00:42:20,801 --> 00:42:25,480
about the life of the Athenians over thousands of years.

469
00:42:26,500 --> 00:42:29,600
The opening of the system was the proudest moment for all of us

470
00:42:29,601 --> 00:42:32,380
when you see the acceptance of the Greek public

